course: dictionary
lesson: 6946
title: Paintbrush: a tool for paint
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A paintbrush is a brush used for putting paint on paper, walls or other surfaces. The word is two words joined together: paint and brush. When you meet a word like this, look at its parts first.
explain: Context clues help too. «Paintbrushes are washed straight after painting, because dry paint goes hard and ruins them.» The clue is dry paint going hard, so a paintbrush must be something that holds wet paint and needs cleaning.
explain: Art and DIY both use paintbrushes, but the sizes change the job. In art, thick brushes cover big areas and thin brushes are for details. For walls and fences, a big paintbrush or roller is used. Always check the sentence around the word to see which kind is meant.
fact: A paintbrush is a brush used for putting paint on paper, walls or other surfaces.
fact: «Paintbrush» is a noun, and its plural is paintbrushes.
fact: In art lessons, thick brushes are for big areas and thin brushes are for details.
fact: Rinse your paintbrush in water between colours, and clean it at the end.
fact: «Paintbrushes are washed straight after painting, because dry paint goes hard and ruins them.»
exercise: What is a paintbrush? | A pot for holding paint | A roller that squeezes water out of cloth | A tiny brush for cleaning your teeth | *A brush used to put paint on paper, walls or other surfaces
exercise: «Paintbrushes are washed straight after painting, because dry paint goes hard and ruins them.» Why are paintbrushes washed straight away? | To make the paint change colour | To make the bristles shine | To stop them from being used again | *To stop dry paint going hard and ruining them
exercise: Which brush would you use to paint a big area of sky? | A pencil | *A thick paintbrush | A ruler | A thin paintbrush for tiny details
test: «When cleaning paintbrushes, rinse watercolour and acrylic in water, but oil paint needs an adult and a special cleaner.» Which paint needs an adult and a special cleaner? | *Oil paint | Acrylic | Chalk | Watercolour
test: In DIY, what are big paintbrushes or rollers used for? | Tiny pictures in art lessons | Cleaning your teeth | *Walls and fences | Writing letters
exam: Why do artists rinse a paintbrush between colours? | To change the brush into a different shape | To make the brush heavier | *To keep the next colour clean | To make the paint dry faster
